gvs(){return"Almost no days off"},
gvu(){return"Where this number comes from"},
gvE(){return"Average over the last four weeks. Days with strength work count; cardio-only days do not. This is not a goal and not a grade \u2014 just your current rhythm."},
gvD(){return"Active recovery week"},
gvC(){return"Not a pause \u2014 a week at half power: working weights and set counts down 30\u201350%. You can skip strength entirely and keep cardio or any other activity. Muscle recovers faster than joints, ligaments, and tendons \u2014 this week gives them time to catch up."},
gvv(){return"What this means"},
gvm(){return"There are enough rest days between sessions that fatigue does not stack. No need to plan active recovery yet \u2014 at this rhythm the body recovers on its own."},
gvn(){return"A steady rhythm you can live on for years. If the sessions are hard, take an active recovery week every few months \u2014 better early than after the weights stall."},
gvl(){return"Sessions sit close together, and fatigue builds quietly. An active recovery week every two or three months usually keeps progress better than grinding without a break."},
gvo(){return"Almost every day under load. That can work if sleep and food keep up. At this rhythm an active recovery week about once a month is ordinary care for joints and ligaments, not a cop-out."},
gvx(){return"This is the rhythm people keep for years."},
gvw(){return"You train often \u2014 a schedule like that does not happen by itself."},
gvy(){return"Looks like you found the body's secret mode."},
gvB(){return"Load already dropped recently \u2014 active recovery can count as done. Pick the pace back up."},
